[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Channels and Synchronization in Go (Golang)"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Go concurrency is built on the philosophy of communicating by sharing memory, rather than sharing memory by communicating. At the heart of this philosophy are channels, which provide a typed conduit through which you can synchronize execution and pass messages between concurrent goroutines."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding Channel Basics"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"A channel in Go is a reference type created using the make function. Channels must be created before use, similarly to maps and slices. Operations on channels are blocking by default, which is what makes them ideal for synchronization without explicit locks or condition variables."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"ch := make(chan int) // Unbuffered channel of integers"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"Unbuffered Channels and Blocking Semantics"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"An unbuffered channel has no capacity. Sending data to an unbuffered channel blocks the sending goroutine until another goroutine receives from that channel. Similarly, receiving from an unbuffered channel blocks until another goroutine sends data. This inherent blocking guarantees synchronization between two execution paths."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"package main

import (
	"fmt"
	"time"
)

func worker(done chan bool) {
	fmt.Println("Working...")
	time.Sleep(time.Second)
	fmt.Println("Done!")

	// Send signal to the main goroutine
	getDone := true
	done <- getDone
}

func main() {
	// Create an unbuffered channel
	done := make(chan bool)

	// Launch worker goroutine
	go worker(done)

	// Block until the worker sends a value
	<-done
	fmt.Println("Main execution completed.")
}"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"Buffered Channels"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"Buffered channels allow you to store a fixed number of values without a corresponding receiver. You specify the capacity as the second argument to the make function. A send operation on a buffered channel blocks only when the buffer is full. A receive operation blocks only when the buffer is empty."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"package main

import "fmt"

func main() {
	// Create a buffered channel with capacity of 2
	msgs := make(chan string, 2)

	// These sends do not block because buffer size is 2
	msgs <- "buffered"
	msgs <- "channel"

	// Receive values
	fmt.Println(<-msgs)
	fmt.Println(<-msgs)
}"},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"Key Synchronization Patterns"},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Wait for Goroutine Completion: Using a channel to signal when a background task has finished, replacing the older time.Sleep approach."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Range over Channels: Using for v := range ch to continuously read from a channel until it is closed by the sender."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Directional Channels: Constraining channels to send-only (chan<-) or receive-only (<-chan) in function signatures to enforce clean API design."},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"Channels in Go provide a robust and elegant mechanism for both data transfer and thread synchronization. By understanding unbuffered versus buffered behaviors, developers can write highly concurrent applications without running into complex race conditions or deadlocks."}]
